    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "Many mini features can be disabled using the same flag pattern by using `vim.g.<minifeature>_disable` or `vim.b.<minifeature>_disable>` depending on the scope (Global or buffer-wise). When **any scope** is set to `true` the feature is disabled.\r\n\r\nI would like to suggest that buffer variables takes precedence over global variables.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n| Global | Buffer | Current behavior | Suggested behavior |\r\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | \r\n| nil | nil | enabled | enabled |\r\n| nil | true | disabled | disabled |\r\n| nil | false | enabled | enabled |\r\n| true | nil | disabled | disabled |\r\n| true | true | disabled | disabled |\r\n| true | false | disabled | **enabled** |\r\n| false | nil | enabled | enabled |\r\n| false | false | enabled | enabled |\r\n| false | true | disabled | disabled |\r\n\r\nThis could easily be done using `vim.F.if_nil`\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nH.is_disabled = function() return vim.F.if_nil(vim.b.miniindentscope_disable, vim.g.miniindentscope_disable, false) end\r\n```\r\n\r\nI suggested this, because I particularly would like indentscope to be disabled by default, and enable only on buffers that I want, so I disable it globally after `setup()` and would change the buffer flag on the buffers I want using a command or keymap.\r\n",
    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Many mini features can be disabled using the same flag pattern by using <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.g.&lt;minifeature&gt;_disable</code> or <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.b.&lt;minifeature&gt;_disable&gt;</code> depending on the scope (Global or buffer-wise). When <strong>any scope</strong> is set to <code class=\"notranslate\">true</code> the feature is disabled.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I would like to suggest that buffer variables takes precedence over global variables.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">For example:</p>\n<markdown-accessiblity-table><table role=\"table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\">Global</th>\n<th align=\"center\">Buffer</th>\n<th align=\"center\">Current behavior</th>\n<th align=\"center\">Suggested behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">nil</td>\n<td align=\"center\">nil</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">nil</td>\n<td align=\"center\">true</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">nil</td>\n<td align=\"center\">false</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">true</td>\n<td align=\"center\">nil</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">true</td>\n<td align=\"center\">true</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">true</td>\n<td align=\"center\">false</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong>enabled</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">false</td>\n<td align=\"center\">nil</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">false</td>\n<td align=\"center\">false</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">enabled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">false</td>\n<td align=\"center\">true</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n<td align=\"center\">disabled</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This could easily be done using <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.F.if_nil</code></p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"H.is_disabled = function() return vim.F.if_nil(vim.b.miniindentscope_disable, vim.g.miniindentscope_disable, false) end\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-smi\">H</span>.<span class=\"pl-en\">is_disabled</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">F</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">if_nil</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">b</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">miniindentscope_disable</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">g</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">miniindentscope_disable</span>, <span class=\"pl-c1\">false</span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I suggested this, because I particularly would like indentscope to be disabled by default, and enable only on buffers that I want, so I disable it globally after <code class=\"notranslate\">setup()</code> and would change the buffer flag on the buffers I want using a command or keymap.</p>",
    "bodyText": "Many mini features can be disabled using the same flag pattern by using vim.g.<minifeature>_disable or vim.b.<minifeature>_disable> depending on the scope (Global or buffer-wise). When any scope is set to true the feature is disabled.\nI would like to suggest that buffer variables takes precedence over global variables.\nFor example:\n\n\n\nGlobal\nBuffer\nCurrent behavior\nSuggested behavior\n\n\n\n\nnil\nnil\nenabled\nenabled\n\n\nnil\ntrue\ndisabled\ndisabled\n\n\nnil\nfalse\nenabled\nenabled\n\n\ntrue\nnil\ndisabled\ndisabled\n\n\ntrue\ntrue\ndisabled\ndisabled\n\n\ntrue\nfalse\ndisabled\nenabled\n\n\nfalse\nnil\nenabled\nenabled\n\n\nfalse\nfalse\nenabled\nenabled\n\n\nfalse\ntrue\ndisabled\ndisabled\n\n\n\nThis could easily be done using vim.F.if_nil\nH.is_disabled = function() return vim.F.if_nil(vim.b.miniindentscope_disable, vim.g.miniindentscope_disable, false) end\nI suggested this, because I particularly would like indentscope to be disabled by default, and enable only on buffers that I want, so I disable it globally after setup() and would change the buffer flag on the buffers I want using a command or keymap.",
